In short: use it, read it, modify it, redistribute it — but nobody may sell it.
The Commons Clause is what removes the right to sell, which the plain MIT text
grants. Deleting the Commons Clause section (everything from the heading
`"Commons Clause" License Condition v1.0` to the end of this file) leaves a
plain, OSI-approved MIT license.

The Commons Clause is NOT an open-source license in the OSI sense: the Open
Source Definition forbids restricting fields of use, and "may not sell" is such
a restriction. That is a deliberate trade-off, not an oversight. Canonical text
of the condition: https://commonsclause.com/

